# Expectation record for 10_declarations_and_types/005_struct_copy_and_return.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_struct_copy_and_return
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = By-value aggregate copy, parameter passing and return across two sizes
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every member is assigned before it is read, so no indeterminate value and no padding byte
is ever observed; aggregates are compared member by member rather than by object
representation.  All member values stay inside the range of their types.  How either
aggregate travels is the ABI's decision, and both sizes are printed rather than assumed so
they are compared as well.  Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
small_folded=1 2
small_runtime=4 5
large_folded=1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
large_runtime=4 5 6 7 8 9 10 11
copy=1 2
passthrough=2 4 unchanged=1 2
consume=10 26
sizes=8 32
END
